// lsp.rpc: the base protocol of the language server -- the framing over a pair
// of descriptors, and the JSON-RPC 2.0 records a frame carries.
//
// A frame is a header block, a `\r\n\r\n`, and exactly `Content-Length` bytes
// of UTF-8 JSON (LSP 3.17, Base Protocol). A conn reads frames from one
// descriptor and writes them to another, so the server is driven by a pipe in
// a test exactly as it is by an editor.
//
// A header field carries no bare carriage return and no bare line feed, and
// its name carries no byte at or below a space -- the C0 controls, NUL and the
// tab among them, and the space itself. A field that breaks either rule is
// refused, and the frame with it. LSP states no rule for either, and this is
// the strict reading of the two the module could take: what matters is that no
// other reader of the same block can cut it into different fields.
//
// Both rules answer one shape. A reader that ends a header line at a line feed
// alone sees two `Content-Length` fields in
// `Content-Length: 5\r\nX: a\nContent-Length: 2`, where this module sees one
// field whose value holds a line feed. A reader that trims a field name reads
// `Content-Length : 48` above a `Content-Length: 2` as one 48-byte body, where
// this module reads two bytes and then a whole second frame; RFC 7230 3.2.4
// makes an HTTP server refuse such a field rather than trim it, and this module
// does the same. Two readers that frame one stream differently is how a message
// smuggled inside another reaches a server.
//
// The name rule is a byte class and not the two bytes the RFC names, because
// the second reader that matters is written in JavaScript.
// `String.prototype.trim` strips the Unicode White_Space set, U+000B and U+000C
// among it, so a vertical tab before the colon is that same smuggle one byte
// over for `vscode-jsonrpc` and for anything else on that runtime. A class
// closed against the RFC's whitespace and open against the reference
// implementation is the wrong way round. No name of the base protocol and no
// HTTP token character lives at or below a space, so the wider class refuses
// nothing a client writes.
//
// ---- what bounds this module ----
//
// An allocation that fails is a runtime error and ends the program (D10.2), so
// every buffer a client can grow needs a bound. There are two. A header block
// above RPC_MAX_HEADER is refused before it is buffered, which stops a client
// that never sends the empty line from growing the read buffer without limit.
// A `Content-Length` above the conn's cap is refused before a byte of the body
// is read.
//
// The body cap is the reader's cap as well: the server calls
// json.set_max_bytes with the number max_content answers, so no body that
// passed the framing is refused for its size by the reader (lsp.json). One
// number, in one place, for one bound.
//
// A frame above the cap ends the loop rather than being skipped. The server
// cannot both bound its memory and stay in step with the stream: the bytes it
// would have to skip are the ones it refuses to read. The cap is sixteen
// mebibytes, above any message an editor sends, so a frame above it is a
// client this server cannot follow.
//
// ---- what an error is ----
//
// Everything a client can get wrong is an error record on the conn -- a flag
// and a message -- and never a panic, as lsp.json answers a malformed document
// with a record. A violated precondition of this module's own API is a panic
// (D13.3), and this module states two: a cap above RPC_CONTENT_CEILING handed
// to set_max_content, and a read size above RPC_CHUNK_CEILING handed to
// set_chunk. Both bound what one call asks the allocator for, and
// test/fort/lsp_rpc_cap_panic_test.ft and test/fort/lsp_rpc_chunk_panic_test.ft
// break one each. `grep -c '^ *panic("' src/lsp/rpc.ft` reads 2.
//
// The end of the stream is not an error. `eof` says the client closed the
// pipe, which the server reads as `exit` reads it.
//
// A write that fails is an error record as well, and one failing write is
// outside that rule: a write to a pipe whose reader alone has closed raises
// SIGPIPE, which ends the program before write_all can answer. A shell reads
// that as status 141 and not as the 1 an orderly end answers with.
//
// The server can meet it. A client that dies after the server has read a whole
// frame into its buffer leaves read_frame able to answer that frame, and the
// response to it hits the closed pipe. The common order is the other one --
// the client closes both ends, read_frame answers the end of the stream, and
// the loop ends before any write -- but it is not the only one.
//
// Nothing here installs a handler, and the language does not stand in the way
// of one: D3.14 allows a cast between a pointer and a u64 and between a
// function pointer and a void*, so `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN)` is expressible
// and D9.8 allows the extern that declares it. A ticket that wants a failed
// write to be an error record rather than a signal installs it in the entry
// file, where a process-wide disposition belongs.
//
// ---- what a view lives for ----
//
// read_frame answers a view into the conn's own read buffer, and the next call
// to read_frame drops the frame it answered. Every id and every method of the
// record classify builds is a view into the document the caller parsed, which
// json.clear ends at the next parse. A caller that keeps either past the next
// message copies it.
//
// Re-entrancy: a conn is a value the caller owns and frees; no module-level
// state.
// D20.5
//
// Dispatch: none. The header block is a loop over lines and the message a
// chain of comparisons, not a table of function pointers.
// D3.10
import lsp.json;
import lsp.text;
import std.io;
import std.str;
import std.strbuf;

/// The bytes one read(2) asks for. A conn takes more in one call only by
/// asking again, so a test that sets `chunk` to 1 proves that a frame is a
/// function of the bytes and not of the read boundaries.
u64 RPC_CHUNK = 4096;

/// The longest header block a frame may carry. A client that never sends the
/// empty line would otherwise grow the read buffer without limit; eight
/// kibibytes is about a hundred times the two fields LSP defines.
u64 RPC_MAX_HEADER = 8192;

/// The longest body a frame may carry, which is lsp.json's own default: a
/// message carries one source file at its largest.
u64 RPC_MAX_CONTENT = 16777216;

/// The largest read size set_chunk takes. A read asks std.strbuf to reserve
/// that many bytes of spare room whatever the stream holds, and an allocation
/// that fails ends the program (D10.2), so the read size needs a bound as the
/// body does. Sixteen mebibytes is the largest body a frame carries by
/// default, and a read larger than the largest frame buys nothing.
/// It bounds the setter and not the field: `chunk` is public and a caller
/// could write it directly, outside this bound. No module of this program
/// does, and set_chunk is the only door this module opens, which is what
/// read_frame says of max_content where the two are summed.
u64 RPC_CHUNK_CEILING = 16777216;

/// The largest cap set_max_content takes, which is the reader's own ceiling:
/// the server hands the reader the number this conn answers, and a document
/// holds the offset of its text in a u32 (lsp.json). It is also what keeps the
/// sum of a header and a body inside a u64 in read_frame, since a cap of the
/// largest u64 would make that sum overflow and trap (D11.1).
/// It bounds the setter and not the field, as RPC_CHUNK_CEILING does:
/// `max_content` is public, and read_frame says the same where it sums.
u64 RPC_CONTENT_CEILING = 4294967295;

/// The header field that gives the length of the body, and the one separator
/// of the base protocol.
string CONTENT_LENGTH = "Content-Length";
string HEADER_END = "\r\n\r\n";
u64 HEADER_END_LEN = 4;
u64 LINE_END_LEN = 2;

/// The base of the decimal number a header field carries.
u64 DECIMAL = 10;

/// The error codes of JSON-RPC 2.0 6 and of LSP 3.17, ErrorCodes. The server
/// answers with four of them today -- ERROR_PARSE, ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST,
/// ERROR_METHOD_NOT_FOUND and ERROR_SERVER_NOT_INITIALIZED, which
/// `grep -n 'rpc.ERROR_' src/lsp/server.ft` finds -- and with no code that is
/// not named here. The other three are the ones the handlers of the later
/// tickets need: a request whose parameters are wrong, a handler that failed,
/// and a request the client cancelled.
i64 ERROR_PARSE = -32700;
i64 ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST = -32600;
i64 ERROR_METHOD_NOT_FOUND = -32601;
i64 ERROR_INVALID_PARAMS = -32602;
i64 ERROR_INTERNAL = -32603;
i64 ERROR_SERVER_NOT_INITIALIZED = -32002;
i64 ERROR_REQUEST_CANCELLED = -32800;

/// How a message names a request. JSON-RPC 2.0 allows a number or a string,
/// and LSP 3.17 allows those two; id_absent is a message that names none,
/// which is a notification, and it is also what the server answers with when
/// it could not read an id at all.
enum id_kind {
    id_absent,
    id_int,
    id_str,
}

/// The id of a request. `num` holds an id_int and `text` an id_str; `text` is
/// a view into the document the id was read from.
struct msg_id {
    id_kind kind;
    i64 num;
    string text;
}

/// What a frame carried. m_invalid is a document this module could not read as
/// a JSON-RPC message, and `error_msg` says which rule it broke; the id is
/// still filled in when one could be read, so the answer names the request the
/// client asked about.
enum msg_kind {
    m_invalid,
    m_request,
    m_notification,
    m_response,
}

/// One message. `method` and `params` are empty on a response; `params` is the
/// handle of the params value in the caller's document, and 0 when the message
/// carries none. Every string here is a view into that document.
struct message {
    msg_kind kind;
    msg_id id;
    string method;
    u32 params;
    string error_msg;
}

/// A pair of descriptors and the buffers of the frames that cross them. conn{}
/// is not usable: create names the descriptors. The conn does not own the
/// descriptors and never closes them, since the server's are stdin and stdout.
struct conn {
    i32 in_fd;
    i32 out_fd;
    strbuf.str_buf in_buf;
    strbuf.str_buf out_buf;
    /// The bytes of in_buf the last frame took, dropped at the next read.
    u64 consumed;
    /// 0 for the defaults, RPC_CHUNK and RPC_MAX_CONTENT, so that conn{} and
    /// create both carry them.
    u64 chunk;
    u64 max_content;
    bool eof;
    bool failed;
    string error_msg;
}

/// A conn over the two descriptors, with the default chunk and cap.
fn conn create(i32 in_fd, i32 out_fd) {
    conn mut c = conn{};
    c.in_fd = in_fd;
    c.out_fd = out_fd;
    return c;
}

/// Releases both buffers and clears the error record; the conn is empty and
/// usable afterwards. The descriptors, the read size and the cap are the
/// caller's and stay as they were, as json.free leaves a document's cap.
/// free of conn{} is a no-op.
fn void free(conn mut* c) {
    strbuf.free(&c->in_buf);
    strbuf.free(&c->out_buf);
    c->consumed = 0;
    c->eof = false;
    c->failed = false;
    c->error_msg = "";
}

/// The bytes one read asks for.
fn u64 chunk_of(conn* c) {
    if (c->chunk == 0) {
        return RPC_CHUNK;
    }
    return c->chunk;
}

/// The longest body this conn accepts.
fn u64 max_content(conn* c) {
    if (c->max_content == 0) {
        return RPC_MAX_CONTENT;
    }
    return c->max_content;
}

/// Moves the read size, 0 restoring RPC_CHUNK. A test sets it to 1 to split
/// every frame across as many reads as it has bytes. A size above
/// RPC_CHUNK_CEILING is a violated precondition and panics (D13.3), as a cap
/// above its own ceiling does: both numbers bound what one call asks the
/// allocator for.
fn void set_chunk(conn mut* c, u64 size) {
    if (size > RPC_CHUNK_CEILING) {
        panic("rpc.set_chunk: above the ceiling");
    }
    c->chunk = size;
}

/// Moves the cap on a body, 0 restoring RPC_MAX_CONTENT. The server hands the
/// reader the same number, so that the two layers cannot disagree about what
/// is too large. A cap above RPC_CONTENT_CEILING is a violated precondition
/// and panics (D13.3): the reader refuses it for the same reason, and the sum
/// of a header and a body must stay inside a u64.
fn void set_max_content(conn mut* c, u64 limit) {
    if (limit > RPC_CONTENT_CEILING) {
        panic("rpc.set_max_content: above the ceiling");
    }
    c->max_content = limit;
}

/// Whether the last read ended because the client closed the stream.
fn bool at_eof(conn* c) {
    return c->eof;
}

/// Whether the conn met an error, and which rule it broke; the message is
/// empty when it met none.
fn bool has_failed(conn* c) {
    return c->failed;
}

fn string error_message(conn* c) {
    return c->error_msg;
}

/// Records the first rule a conn broke. A conn that failed reads no further
/// frame, since it no longer knows where one begins.
fn void fail(conn mut* c, string message) {
    if (!c->failed) {
        c->failed = true;
        c->error_msg = message;
    }
}

/// The bytes read and not yet consumed.
fn u64 buffered(conn* c) {
    return c->in_buf.len;
}

/// Drops the frame the last read answered, which is what keeps the buffer at
/// the size of the frames in flight rather than of the session. The tail moves
/// down one byte at a time: it is the bytes of the next frame that a read
/// brought in early, and never a whole message.
fn void drop_consumed(conn mut* c) {
    if (c->consumed == 0) {
        return;
    }
    u64 rest = c->in_buf.len - c->consumed;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < rest) {
        c->in_buf.data[i] = c->in_buf.data[c->consumed + i];
        i += 1;
    }
    strbuf.truncate(&c->in_buf, rest);
    c->consumed = 0;
}

/// One read into the spare room of the read buffer. False at the end of the
/// stream and on a read error, which has_failed tells apart.
fn bool fill(conn mut* c) {
    u64 size = chunk_of(c);
    strbuf.reserve(&c->in_buf, size);
    // The read lands in the buffer's own storage, so nothing is copied
    // afterwards; the room is `size` bytes and not the whole spare capacity,
    // which is what makes the read size the one set_chunk fixed.
    u8 mut@ room = c->in_buf.data[c->in_buf.len..c->in_buf.len + size];
    i64 n = io.read(c->in_fd, room);
    if (n < 0) {
        fail(c, "the stream could not be read");
        return false;
    }
    if (n == 0) {
        c->eof = true;
        return false;
    }
    c->in_buf.len += cast(n, u64);
    return true;
}

/// The offset just past the `\r\n\r\n` that ends the header block, or 0 when
/// the buffer does not hold one yet. `from` is where to start looking, which
/// the caller moves forward so that a header is scanned once.
fn u64 header_end(conn* c, u64 mut* from) {
    string buffered_text = strbuf.view(&c->in_buf);
    if (buffered_text.len >= LINE_END_LEN && buffered_text[0..LINE_END_LEN] == "\r\n") {
        // The block ends at its first empty line, so a block with no field at
        // all is two bytes. It declares no length and content_length refuses
        // it; without this case the scan below would run on into the frame
        // after it and read two blocks as one.
        return LINE_END_LEN;
    }
    if (buffered_text.len < HEADER_END_LEN) {
        return 0;
    }
    u64 mut at = *from;
    u64 last = buffered_text.len - HEADER_END_LEN;
    while (at <= last) {
        if (buffered_text[at..at + HEADER_END_LEN] == HEADER_END) {
            return at + HEADER_END_LEN;
        }
        at += 1;
    }
    // The next scan starts where a terminator could still begin.
    *from = at;
    return 0;
}

/// The value of a header field, without the spaces around it. The protocol
/// writes one space after the colon; a client that writes none, or several, is
/// read the same way.
fn string trimmed(string s) {
    u64 mut from = 0;
    while (from < s.len && (s[from] == ' ' || s[from] == '\t')) {
        from += 1;
    }
    u64 mut to = s.len;
    while (to > from && (s[to - 1] == ' ' || s[to - 1] == '\t')) {
        to -= 1;
    }
    return s[from..to];
}

/// Whether `s` holds a byte at or below a space: the C0 controls, NUL and the
/// tab among them, and the space itself. A field name may hold none of them.
/// D3.2: char is ordered by unsigned byte value
fn bool holds_space_or_control(string s) {
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < s.len) {
        if (s[i] <= ' ') {
            return true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return false;
}

/// Reads the header block, which ends at `end`, and answers the body length it
/// declares. False with an error record when a field is malformed, when a
/// field holds a bare carriage return or a bare line feed, when a field name
/// holds a byte at or below a space, when the block declares no length, or
/// when it declares two that differ.
fn bool content_length(conn mut* c, u64 end, u64 mut* out) {
    string block = strbuf.view(&c->in_buf)[0..end];
    bool mut found = false;
    u64 mut at = 0;
    while (at + LINE_END_LEN <= block.len) {
        u64 mut stop = at;
        while (stop + LINE_END_LEN <= block.len
               && !(block[stop] == '\r' && block[stop + 1] == '\n')) {
            stop += 1;
        }
        if (stop == at) {
            // The empty line that ends the block.
            break;
        }
        string line = block[at..stop];
        if (str.index_of(line, '\r') >= 0 || str.index_of(line, '\n') >= 0) {
            // A field whose bytes hold a bare carriage return or a bare line
            // feed is refused, and the frame with it. A reader that ends a
            // line at either byte reads fields this one does not:
            // `Content-Length: 5\r\nX: a\nContent-Length: 2\r\n\r\n` is one
            // five-byte body here and two declared lengths there, which is
            // where a message smuggled inside another gets in. A field of the
            // base protocol ends at `\r\n` and the block at `\r\n\r\n`, so
            // neither byte stands inside a well-formed field.
            fail(c, "a header field holds a bare carriage return or line feed");
            return false;
        }
        i64 colon = str.index_of(line, ':');
        if (colon < 0) {
            fail(c, "a header field has no colon");
            return false;
        }
        string name = line[0..cast(colon, u64)];
        if (holds_space_or_control(name)) {
            // A field name that holds a byte at or below a space is refused,
            // and the frame with it, for the reason the two bytes above are.
            // This module compares the name as it stands, so
            // `Content-Length : 48` names no field it reads, while a reader
            // that trims the name cuts the same block into other fields. The
            // review measured what that costs on a block of
            // `Content-Length : 48` above a `Content-Length: 2`: this module
            // read a two-byte body and then a whole second frame, and a
            // trimming reader reads one 48-byte body that swallows the second
            // message. It measured the same smuggle again with a vertical tab
            // in place of the space, which is why the class is every byte at
            // or below a space and not the two the RFC names; the header says
            // why. The name needs no trimming after this, which is why it gets
            // none.
            fail(c, "a header field name holds a space or a control byte");
            return false;
        }
        string value = trimmed(line[cast(colon, u64) + 1..]);
        if (text.equal_no_case(name, CONTENT_LENGTH)) {
            u64 mut declared = 0;
            if (!str.parse_u64(value, DECIMAL, &declared)) {
                fail(c, "the Content-Length is not a number");
                return false;
            }
            if (found && declared != *out) {
                // Two lengths that differ leave no reading of where the body
                // ends, so the stream cannot be followed past this frame.
                fail(c, "the frame declares two lengths");
                return false;
            }
            *out = declared;
            found = true;
        }
        at = stop + LINE_END_LEN;
    }
    if (!found) {
        fail(c, "the frame has no Content-Length");
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// The body of the next frame, as a view into the conn's read buffer that
/// lives until the next call. False at the end of the stream (at_eof) and on a
/// malformed frame (has_failed); a conn that failed answers false ever after.
fn bool read_frame(conn mut* c, string mut* out) {
    *out = "";
    drop_consumed(c);
    if (c->failed || c->eof) {
        return false;
    }
    u64 mut from = 0;
    u64 mut end = header_end(c, &from);
    while (end == 0) {
        if (buffered(c) > RPC_MAX_HEADER) {
            fail(c, "the header is too long");
            return false;
        }
        if (!fill(c)) {
            if (c->failed) {
                return false;
            }
            if (buffered(c) == 0) {
                // The client closed the stream between two frames, which is
                // the one end that is not an error.
                return false;
            }
            fail(c, "the stream ended inside a header");
            return false;
        }
        end = header_end(c, &from);
    }
    if (end > RPC_MAX_HEADER) {
        // The block ended, and it is longer than the bound: the test above
        // holds the buffer of a client that sends no terminator, and this one
        // holds the block itself, so the bound reads the same whatever the
        // read size was.
        fail(c, "the header is too long");
        return false;
    }
    u64 mut len = 0;
    if (!content_length(c, end, &len)) {
        return false;
    }
    if (len > max_content(c)) {
        fail(c, "the frame is too large");
        return false;
    }
    // The sum fits. `end` is at most RPC_MAX_HEADER, which the test above has
    // just enforced, and `len` is at most the cap, which every call of
    // set_max_content holds to RPC_CONTENT_CEILING. The cap is a field and a
    // caller could write it directly, outside that bound; no module of this
    // program does, and set_max_content is the only door this module opens.
    // D11.1
    u64 total = end + len;
    while (buffered(c) < total) {
        if (!fill(c)) {
            if (!c->failed) {
                fail(c, "the stream ended inside the body");
            }
            return false;
        }
    }
    *out = strbuf.view(&c->in_buf)[end..total];
    c->consumed = total;
    return true;
}

/// Writes one frame: the header, the empty line and the body, in one write, so
/// that a client never reads a header without its body. False when the write
/// failed, with the reason in the error record.
fn bool write_frame(conn mut* c, string body) {
    strbuf.clear(&c->out_buf);
    strbuf.append(&c->out_buf, CONTENT_LENGTH);
    strbuf.append(&c->out_buf, ": ");
    strbuf.append_u64(&c->out_buf, body.len);
    strbuf.append(&c->out_buf, HEADER_END);
    strbuf.append(&c->out_buf, body);
    if (!io.write_all(c->out_fd, strbuf.bytes(&c->out_buf))) {
        fail(c, "the stream could not be written");
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// ---- the message -----------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether the two ids name one request. Two absent ids name none, so they are
/// never the same: a cancel that carries no id cancels nothing.
fn bool same_id(msg_id a, msg_id b) {
    if (a.kind != b.kind) {
        return false;
    }
    if (a.kind == id_kind.id_int) {
        return a.num == b.num;
    }
    if (a.kind == id_kind.id_str) {
        return a.text == b.text;
    }
    return false;
}

/// The id a message member carries, absent when the member is missing, null,
/// or a value JSON-RPC does not allow there. `ok` is false in that last case,
/// which is a message this module refuses.
fn msg_id id_of(json.doc* d, u32 v, bool mut* ok) {
    msg_id mut id = msg_id{};
    *ok = true;
    if (v == 0) {
        return id;
    }
    json.value_kind kind = json.kind_of(d, v);
    if (kind == json.value_kind.k_int) {
        id.kind = id_kind.id_int;
        id.num = json.int_of(d, v);
        return id;
    }
    if (kind == json.value_kind.k_string) {
        id.kind = id_kind.id_str;
        id.text = json.text_of(d, v);
        return id;
    }
    if (kind == json.value_kind.k_null) {
        // JSON-RPC 2.0 answers an unreadable request with a null id, and LSP
        // 3.17 forbids one on a request. A message that carries it names no
        // request, so it is read as a notification.
        return id;
    }
    *ok = false;
    return id;
}

/// Reads the parsed document as a JSON-RPC message. The document must be one
/// the caller parsed; a document that failed to parse has no root and is
/// m_invalid. Every string of the record is a view into that document.
fn void classify(json.doc* d, message mut* out) {
    *out = message{};
    u32 root = json.root(d);
    if (root == 0 || json.kind_of(d, root) != json.value_kind.k_object) {
        // An array here is a JSON-RPC batch, which LSP does not use.
        out->error_msg = "a message is a JSON object";
        return;
    }
    // The id is read before every other rule, so that the answer to a message
    // this module refuses names the request the client asked about. JSON-RPC
    // 2.0 5 asks for a null id only where the id itself could not be read.
    bool mut ok = true;
    out->id = id_of(d, json.member_of(d, root, "id"), &ok);
    if (!ok) {
        out->error_msg = "an id is a number or a string";
        return;
    }
    u32 version = json.member_of(d, root, "jsonrpc");
    if (version == 0 || json.kind_of(d, version) != json.value_kind.k_string
        || json.text_of(d, version) != "2.0") {
        out->error_msg = "a message carries jsonrpc 2.0";
        return;
    }
    u32 method = json.member_of(d, root, "method");
    if (method != 0) {
        if (json.kind_of(d, method) != json.value_kind.k_string) {
            out->error_msg = "a method is a string";
            return;
        }
        out->method = json.text_of(d, method);
        out->params = json.member_of(d, root, "params");
        out->kind = msg_kind.m_notification;
        if (out->id.kind != id_kind.id_absent) {
            out->kind = msg_kind.m_request;
        }
        return;
    }
    if (json.member_of(d, root, "result") != 0 || json.member_of(d, root, "error") != 0) {
        // An answer to a request this server sent. It sends none yet, and a
        // response is never answered in any case (JSON-RPC 2.0 5).
        out->kind = msg_kind.m_response;
        return;
    }
    out->error_msg = "a message has a method, a result or an error";
}

// ---- the answers -----------------------------------------------------------------

/// Writes the id as the value of the `id` member: the number, the string, or
/// null for an absent one, which is what JSON-RPC 2.0 5 answers a request it
/// could not read with.
fn void write_id(json.writer mut* w, msg_id id) {
    if (id.kind == id_kind.id_int) {
        json.write_int(w, id.num);
        return;
    }
    if (id.kind == id_kind.id_str) {
        json.write_str(w, id.text);
        return;
    }
    json.write_null(w);
}

/// Opens a successful response and leaves the writer where the result value
/// goes. The caller writes exactly one value and then calls response_end.
fn void response_begin(json.writer mut* w, msg_id id) {
    json.write_object_begin(w);
    json.write_key(w, "jsonrpc");
    json.write_str(w, "2.0");
    json.write_key(w, "id");
    write_id(w, id);
    json.write_key(w, "result");
}

/// Closes the object response_begin opened.
fn void response_end(json.writer mut* w) {
    json.write_object_end(w);
}

/// The whole of an error response. `message` is the sentence a client shows a
/// user, so it names what the server could not do and not where in the code it
/// gave up.
fn void write_error(json.writer mut* w, msg_id id, i64 code, string message) {
    json.write_object_begin(w);
    json.write_key(w, "jsonrpc");
    json.write_str(w, "2.0");
    json.write_key(w, "id");
    write_id(w, id);
    json.write_key(w, "error");
    json.write_object_begin(w);
    json.write_key(w, "code");
    json.write_int(w, code);
    json.write_key(w, "message");
    json.write_str(w, message);
    json.write_object_end(w);
    json.write_object_end(w);
}
